    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d303">

                <pb facs="0654" n="642" sameAs="frus:frus1946v09/pg642"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Marshall Mission Files, Lot 54–D270</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by General Marshall to Generalissimo Chiang
                        Kai-shek</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-04-19">April 19, 1946</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>During my absence the Committee of Three dispatched a delegation to Canton to
                    assist in resolving the Communist problem in that area. I have just read a
                    report made by the American member of that delegation<anchor n="97"
                        xml:id="d303-f1"/> and also your instructions to General Chang Fa-kwei dated
                    5 April 1946<anchor n="98" xml:id="d303-f2"/> which resolves the major portion
                    of the difficulties that existed prior to the visit by the delegation.</p>
                <p>However there remains one aspect of the matter which still requires corrective
                    action. General Chang Fa-kwei continues in his attitude of non-recognition of
                    Communist Forces in Kwan[g]tung Province, which is not in keeping with the
                    spirit of the 10 January Cease Fire agreement.</p>
                <p>I intend bringing up this entire question to the Committee of Three at an early
                    date. Prior to doing so I would greatly appreciate your comments in the
                    matter.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d303-f1" n="97" xml:id="d303-f1-content">
                <p>See telegram of April 2 from Col. J. Hart Caughey to Brig. Gen. Henry A. Byroade,
                        <ref target="#pg636">p. 636</ref>, the most comprehensive report to be sent
                    on this problem.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d303-f2" n="98" xml:id="d303-f2-content">
                <p>See memorandum by Col. J. Hart Caughey to Mr. Liao Cheng-chi, April 9, <ref
                        target="#pg641">p. 641</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
